{{- /*
claude-settings-validate.tmpl — fail the apply LOUDLY on a bad Claude Code
settings declaration, before the provisioner renders a single line of script.

The config-claude-settings provisioner is this partial's caller. Validation lives
here rather than inline in that script on purpose: a guard written inline can
silently exist for one consumer only.

Every check here aborts the whole chezmoi command. None of them can be satisfied
by a runtime skip, because a declaration this file rejects is wrong on every host.

  1. SHAPE. agents.claude.settings is a map. An EMPTY map is valid and means
     "manage nothing" — the reconciler renders a notice and no assertion loop —
     so it returns here rather than failing. That keeps the declaration's
     pre-adoption state a legal state.
  2. PATH GRAMMAR. Each key is a dot-separated path to a LEAF in settings.json.
     Every dot is a separator, so a segment must be non-empty: a leading,
     trailing, or doubled dot is rejected. Segments admit hyphens and digits
     because model ids are path segments (modelSettings.claude-opus-5.effortLevel).
     A settings key whose OWN name contains a dot has no expressible form here and
     is indistinguishable from an extra segment, so it is out of scope rather than
     detectable — see the plan's Scope Boundaries.
  3. LEAF VALUES ONLY. A declared path names a LEAF. A container value would be
     written wholesale by setpath and would destroy every sibling another writer
     put inside that record -- the exact clobbering this whole design removes.
  4. NO PATH OVERLAP. Two declared paths where one is an ancestor of the other
     pass every per-path check, then the write applies them in sequence against
     progressively mutated state and dies on a scalar it created itself. Under
     `set -e` that aborts the whole apply with a bare jq error naming no path, so
     only this render-time check can report it usefully.
  5. NO OTHER WRITER'S NAMESPACE. hooks belongs to aoe, which injects session
     tracking at session start; enabledPlugins and extraKnownMarketplaces belong
     to install-claude-plugins. Asserting into any of them would fight a writer
     that rewrites the same file during ordinary use, and the loser would be
     whichever ran last. A comment saying "do not declare these" is a convention
     the next edit breaks; this is the check that holds.
  6. SECURITY-SENSITIVE KEYS ARE ALLOWLISTED, NOT BLOCKLISTED. Check 5 screens for
     OWNERSHIP -- who else writes the key. It cannot tell a notification toggle
     from a key that grants a permission, so a permission declaration would enter
     through the same data edit as inputNeededNotifEnabled. This check screens for
     RISK instead: a path under a security-sensitive prefix, or one of the
     security-sensitive exact keys, is accepted ONLY when its full path appears in
     the reviewed allowlist. An allowlist and not a blocklist, because a blocklist
     would need a new entry for every permission key the vendor adds and would
     fail open until someone noticed.

     THE DECLARABLE permissions.* SURFACE IS permissions.defaultMode ALONE, and it
     is deliberately left undeclared -- see AGENTS.md. permissions.allow, .deny and
     .ask are LISTS, and check 3 already rejects a container value; there is no
     narrower form to accept, because owning individual entries would need a path
     syntax for list MEMBERSHIP and the grammar in check 2 addresses object keys
     only. That is a scope boundary, not a gap waiting on a mechanism.
     docs/plans/2026-09-02-1529-feat-reconcile-claude-settings-plan.md is
     authoritative for what already shipped here.

  {{ includeTemplate "claude-settings-validate.tmpl" (dict "ctx" . "settings" $settings) }}
*/ -}}
{{- $ctx := .ctx -}}
{{- if not $ctx -}}{{- fail "claude-settings-validate.tmpl: ctx is required" -}}{{- end -}}
{{- $settings := .settings -}}
{{- if not (kindIs "map" $settings) -}}
{{-   fail (printf "config-claude-settings: agents.claude.settings must be a map, got %s" (kindOf $settings)) -}}
{{- end -}}
{{- /* Segments start with a letter and admit digits, underscore and hyphen. The
   trailing hyphen in the class is a literal, not a range. This regexp rejects an
   empty path, an empty segment, and any segment carrying a character a
   settings.json key cannot hold. */ -}}
{{- $pathRe := "^[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9_-]*([.][A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9_-]*)*$" -}}
{{- $ownedElsewhere := list "hooks" "enabledPlugins" "extraKnownMarketplaces" -}}
{{- /* Check 6's three lists. `$securityPrefixes` holds first segments whose whole
   subtree grants or bypasses a permission; `$securityExactKeys` holds single keys
   that do the same without a prefix to key on; `$reviewedSecurityPaths` is the
   full-path allowlist both are measured against. Adding a path here is a reviewed
   change to this partial, which is the point -- it cannot happen as a data edit. */ -}}
{{- $securityPrefixes := list "permissions" -}}
{{- $securityExactKeys := list "skipDangerousModePermissionPrompt" -}}
{{- $reviewedSecurityPaths := list "permissions.defaultMode" "skipDangerousModePermissionPrompt" -}}
{{- range $path, $value := $settings -}}
{{-   if not (regexMatch $pathRe $path) -}}
{{-     fail (printf "config-claude-settings: %q is not a valid settings path; use dot-separated segments naming a leaf in settings.json, each segment non-empty and starting with a letter" $path) -}}
{{-   end -}}
{{-   if or (kindIs "map" $value) (kindIs "slice" $value) -}}
{{-     fail (printf "config-claude-settings: %s declares a %s value, but a declared path must name a LEAF; asserting a container would replace the whole record and destroy every sibling another writer put there" $path (kindOf $value)) -}}
{{-   end -}}
{{-   $first := index (splitList "." $path) 0 -}}
{{-   if has $first $ownedElsewhere -}}
{{-     fail (printf "config-claude-settings: declared path %s addresses %s, which another writer owns (hooks -> aoe session tracking; enabledPlugins and extraKnownMarketplaces -> install-claude-plugins). Asserting it would fight that writer on every apply" $path $first) -}}
{{-   end -}}
{{-   if and (or (has $first $securityPrefixes) (has $path $securityExactKeys)) (not (has $path $reviewedSecurityPaths)) -}}
{{-     fail (printf "config-claude-settings: declared path %s grants or bypasses a Claude Code permission, and only these paths are reviewed for that: %s. permissions.allow, permissions.deny and permissions.ask are lists, so asserting one would replace the whole list and drop every entry a user or a plugin contributed; owning single entries would need a path syntax for list membership that this grammar does not have. Declare a reviewed path, or leave the key undeclared so it stays adjustable" $path (join ", " $reviewedSecurityPaths)) -}}
{{-   end -}}
{{- end -}}
{{- /* Overlap is checked only after EVERY path has passed the grammar rule above:
   Go template map iteration has no defined order, so a shared pass would let this
   check fire on a malformed path and report the wrong reason. */ -}}
{{- range $path, $value := $settings -}}
{{-   range $other, $_ := $settings -}}
{{-     if and (ne $other $path) (hasPrefix (printf "%s." $path) $other) -}}
{{-       fail (printf "config-claude-settings: declared path %s is an ancestor of %s; only one of them can name a leaf, and declaring both aborts the apply with no usable diagnostic" $path $other) -}}
{{-     end -}}
{{-   end -}}
{{- end -}}
